{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>Eye for eye</b> It is known from the tradition of our Sages that this refers to monetary compensation (Mekhilta here; Bava Kamma 84a), and this same expression will occur in the context of restitution: \"and one who kills a beast shall pay for it, life for life\" (Leviticus 24:18). And R. Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 21:24) said that the intent of the verse is to say that he is liable for this if he does not give his ransom. And the verse forbids us from not accepting ransom for the life of a murderer who is wicked enough to die (Numbers 35:31), but we do accept ransom in the case of one who is wicked enough to have a limb severed from his limbs. Therefore we do not sever it ever, but he pays the value of the damage. And if he does not have it, the damage remains upon him as a debt until his means reach and he redeems himself. And the proof for the words of the Sages is what was said above (Exodus 21:19) <b>only his lost time shall he give and shall cause him to be thoroughly healed</b>, for if we were to do to the man who strikes his fellow as he did to him, what would he pay afterward, when he too requires lost time and healing? And there is no argument on account of one who heals quickly, for that is not the plain sense of the verse. Rather, the verse speaks of every man. And even if he healed faster, we have already taken our vengeance from him, for we did to him exactly as he did.\n\nAnd by way of the plain sense there is no escape from this question, except if they say that the striker who places a mum (blemish) in his fellow (Leviticus 24:19), and it is a permanent mum that will remain in him, such as eye, hand, or foot, and a burn from which the mark remains forever — in that case we do the like to his body, and that is what it says (Leviticus 24:20) \"as he has placed a mum in a man, so shall be placed in him,\" and there are no payments for lost time and healing at all. But one who strikes another with a stone or a fist upon his body and he takes to bed and heals completely and no mum remains in his body — concerning this it says (Exodus 21:19) <b>only his lost time shall he give and shall cause him to be thoroughly healed</b>. Now, the verse that mentioned burn and wound and bruise — according to its meaning, all these damages are included in this category, and the wound and the bruise will heal completely. And what the verse said there (Leviticus 24:19) \"and if a man places a mum in his fellow,\" it intended to include all blows, for there it did not elaborate to mention wound and bruise and burn, and said mum, because every blow produces a mum at its time, and even one that heals is called a mum, as we have said, mum over (a passing blemish, Bekhorot 37a). And the Torah called a scab or eczema or a rubbed sore a mum (Leviticus 21:20), and all of them are passing and heal. And it is written: \"children in whom there is no mum\" (Daniel 1:4). And the principle is that the tradition is true in every respect."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "The paragraph break (marked by ועל דרך הפשט) is preserved as a newline. The phrase מוּם עוֹבֵר is rendered literally with a parenthetical gloss citing Bekhorot 37a, as the argument turns on the technical halakhic category."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:18:25.889875507Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}